  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Scattered afternoon/evening showers/storms. \nSummertime pattern continues into early next week then a cool\ndown.\n\nCold front across the northern Mid-Atlantic is moving south.\nScattered showers/storms precede this boundary and will pass\nacross the forecast area this afternoon and evening with\nmarginal risk for a severe storm or two along and south of the \nthe VA/NC border. Environment across southside VA and into the \ncentral Piedmont of NC features temperatures in the lower 90s \nwith dewpoints near 70. Poor mid-level lapse rates and weak \ndeep-layer flow/shear will tend to limit overall convective \nintensity and organization; however, MLCAPE of 1000-2000 J/kg \ncoupled with steep low-level lapse rates and PW around 1.75-2.00\ninches (as supported by a modified 12z GSO observed sounding \nand current objective analysis) are supportive of at least some \npotential for isolated damaging downburst wind gusts (with peak \ngust magnitudes in the 45-55 mph range). Locally greater \npotential may exist across portions of central North Carolina \nwhere the latest observational trends and CAM guidance are \nsuggestive of greater storm coverage, but overall expect this \nactivity to favor areas mainly south or our CWA courtesy of\nincreasing northerly wind flow with time.\n\nThe front which is forecast to cross the forecast area from\nnorth to south tonight will introduce a modest surge of cooler \nair which will have a stabilizing effect for our weather\nSaturday. Temperatures Saturday are expected to be 10-15 degrees\ncooler with METMOS a full 10 degrees cooler than MAVMOS for\ndaytime highs. Think this is due to what will likely be a day of\npersistent cloud cover (the GFS not capturing the full extent \nof the cloudiness) behind the front with shallow wedge of high \npressure building into the forecast area from the northeast. \nThere is still a chance for showers but mainly during the\nmorning with lack of solar insolation leading to less CAPE in\nthe afternoon. For now will maintain low chance pops, but we\nmay very well squeak by with a dry Saturday. \n\nBy Sunday the flow turns quickly with warm air advection\nadvancing across the TN/OH valleys. This will push the front \nback to the north as a warm front...only to clash with another \nsouthward moving upper level trough Monday and Tuesday, \ntemperatures trending cooler again for mid week. A wholesale\nuptick in rain chances area expected for both Monday and Tuesday\nwith widespread QPF of one inch or more possible during this\ntime frame. 48 hour probabilistic ensemble guidance indicates \n80 percent chance for 0.50, 50% chance of 1.00 and 25% chance of\n1.50 or more. This is good news. For most, this will be much \nneeded rain.",
